WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.480
This episode is sponsored by Blinkist.&nbsp;
Blinkist takes all of the need-to-know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:07.200
information from thousands of nonfiction books&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:15.920
and condenses it down into just 15 minutes and you&nbsp;
can go to blinkist.com/microcosmos to learn more.

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:28.800
Sometimes James, our master of microscopes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:34.240
likes to go through his samples with the lowest&nbsp;
magnification objective, looking at the organisms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:38.880
as they’re floating in a Petri dish so that he&nbsp;
can see everything from a distance and get a sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:45.040
for what mysteries await him when he zooms in.
And during one of his recent Petri dish scans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:50.800
he found this stunning ciliate, autofluorescing&nbsp;
a whole crayon box’s worth of colors under our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:55.280
combination of UV, blue, green, and red light.
And when you switch to white light,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.280 --> 00:01:00.960
the ciliate is still quite colorful. Maybe not&nbsp;
the same fluorescent rainbow it was before, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:05.520
full of varied shades of purple like&nbsp;
a blueberry bush ripening in summer.&nbsp;

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:09.680
But the ciliate didn’t seem to be&nbsp;
doing so well in the Petri dish.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:15.840
So for James to be able to take this video with&nbsp;
the clarity and focus it needs, he first had to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:21.520
move the ciliate to a slide—a procedure that&nbsp;
is quite normal and safe for most microbes.&nbsp;

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:28.240
But for our already dying ciliate, it proved&nbsp;
to be too much. As soon as it hit the slide,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:33.280
it burst open. And all of those little&nbsp;
blueberries came pouring slowly out as well.&nbsp;

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:38.640
The ciliate died too fast for us to be&nbsp;
able to identify it. There was just too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:44.640
much damage, erasing all clues to its identity.
But the ciliate wasn’t the only organism in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:52.160
video. Those round bits of purple were once alive&nbsp;
as well. And what remains of their color provides&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:59.360
more than enough information to identify them.
These are purple sulfur bacteria. And ciliates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:05.440
are not the only danger they face. No, its biggest&nbsp;
foe is something even more pedestrian than that.&nbsp;

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:10.160
You see, something you need to know&nbsp;
about the purple sulfur bacteria&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:14.400
is that it’s a bit of a contrarian,&nbsp;
specifically when it comes to photosynthesis.&nbsp;

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:24.880
Now, what do you think of when you&nbsp;
hear the word “photosynthesis?”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:28.800
You might think of trees and plants and&nbsp;
leaves. Or since you’re here with us right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:33.680
now on this journey, maybe you’re thinking&nbsp;
of algae and cyanobacteria and euglena.&nbsp;

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:40.320
What most of us are probably imagining could boil&nbsp;
down to the color green. Lots and lots of green.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:45.840
And for good reason. Chlorophyll, the pigment that&nbsp;
absorbs light and kicks off the reactions that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:51.680
drive photosynthesis, is green. And that green&nbsp;
has come to dominate the world that we live in.&nbsp;

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:58.080
But there’s another world too,&nbsp;
inhabited by purple sulfur bacteria.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:06.000
And these purple sulfur bacteria can also do&nbsp;
photosynthesis. They have bacteriochlorophyll.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:12.240
But they also contain carotenoids, pigments that&nbsp;
create colors ranging from red to purple, and that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:18.640
help the bacteria harvest light and get protection&nbsp;
from the bacteria’s primary foe: oxygen.&nbsp;

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:26.080
“But wait!” you might be saying to yourself. “How&nbsp;
can an organism be photosynthetic and so afraid of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:34.560
oxygen? Doesn’t photosynthesis create oxygen?”
And yes, you would be correct—most of the time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:41.360
The most common form of photosynthesis in our&nbsp;
world is oxygenic photosynthesis. It makes oxygen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:46.320
This is what happens in all those plants&nbsp;
and cyanobacteria, where energy from light&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:52.000
sets off a chain reaction involving water and&nbsp;
carbon dioxide. The end result is a sugar for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:58.640
the organism and oxygen for the atmosphere. That&nbsp;
is the world we live in, a world where oxygen is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:05.440
necessity and readily supplied by photosynthesis.
But purple sulfur bacteria, contrarian that it is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:11.920
prefers the worlds in this world that do&nbsp;
not have oxygen. Our samples, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:17.760
came from the sediments at the bottom of ponds&nbsp;
and puddles, places where oxygen does not arrive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:24.000
consumed as it has been by the organisms above.
Down at the bottom of their ponds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:30.320
the purple sulfur bacteria do their own form of&nbsp;
photosynthesis called anoxygenic photosynthesis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:34.800
And they’re not the only organisms to prefer&nbsp;
this oxygen-less approach to making their own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:41.840
food—confusingly enough, there are also green&nbsp;
sulfur bacteria and purple non-sulfur bacteria,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:46.800
both of which also do anoxygenic photosynthesis&nbsp;
though sometimes with their own twists.&nbsp;

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:56.000
For the purple sulfur bacteria, the key ingredient&nbsp;
is, well it’s sulfur. You may not think of sulfur&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:00.240
as an essential element to life, but that’s&nbsp;
because we have bodies borne out of a world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:05.760
full of oxygen. But in the stratified world of&nbsp;
the purple sulfur bacteria, the void left by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:11.760
a lack of oxygen instead is filled by sulfur.
So where a normal photosynthetic organism would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:18.240
consume water to drive their photosynthesis, they&nbsp;
consume sulfurous chemicals like hydrogen sulfide,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:22.080
which you might recognize from its rotten&nbsp;
egg smell. And when they’re done making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:27.440
their own food, the byproduct of their&nbsp;
endeavors is not oxygen, it is sulfur.&nbsp;

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:32.320
And because they live in an environment that is&nbsp;
constantly changing, purple sulfur bacteria are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:38.320
able to store up sulfurous molecules for whatever&nbsp;
the microbial equivalent of a rainy day is.&nbsp;

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:43.200
All of this sulfur-based photosynthesis makes&nbsp;
purple sulfur bacteria something better than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:50.000
just contrarian: they are a good neighbor, helping&nbsp;
to consume hydrogen sulfide that might be toxic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:52.960
and clearing out the waters for&nbsp;
their upstairs neighbors that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:59.760
require a more oxygenated environment to survive.
Though that role, that place as a good neighbor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:06.480
is based on the ecological neighborhoods we have&nbsp;
today, where lawns of oxygen give way to lawns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:12.080
of sulfur. That neighborhood has gone through&nbsp;
some changes though, and there was a time before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:18.080
oxygen’s dominance, where purple sulfur bacteria&nbsp;
were less of a contrarian than they are now.&nbsp;

00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:25.760
In 2005, researchers uncovered photosynthetic&nbsp;
pigments, fossilized and preserved in rocks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:32.800
that are more than a billion years old, lying in a&nbsp;
large basin in Australia. These fossils suggested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:38.480
that purple sulfur bacteria may have&nbsp;
existed in ancient oceans during a period&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:44.160
when oxygen was still rising in the world&nbsp;
but had not come to take over the oceans.&nbsp;

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:50.800
The fossils suggest an ancient world that would&nbsp;
be unrecognizable to us. In fact, we probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:56.720
wouldn’t be able to survive there. But as the&nbsp;
world has shifted, the purple sulfur bacteria&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.720 --> 00:07:02.800
found a way to shift with it, to make space&nbsp;
for themselves even as new neighbors move in.&nbsp;

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:08.560
But of course, those other organisms don’t&nbsp;
seem to be looking to thank the purple sulfur&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:15.840
bacteria any time soon. I mean, who needs a&nbsp;
neighbor when you can have a meal instead?&nbsp;

